      "text": "Let $A=(0,0),B=(0,11),$ and $C=(10,11)$ be the vertices of the rectangle . Now consider a right triangle $XYZ$ such that $X$ and $Y$ lie on $CD$ and $AB$ respectively, $Z$ lies on $AD$ and $\\angle{XZD}=60^{\\circ}$. Let $X=(x,0)$ and $Y=(10,11-y)$. Taking the angle between $XZ$ and $YZ$ as $60^{\\circ}$, we have\n\\[\\tau=3\\pi/2\\]\nso\n\\[(10-x)^2+(-11+y)^2=(10-x)\\sqrt{3}(y-11)\\]\nso $y=10+\\sqrt3x$. Now $$[XYZ]=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (10+\\sqrt3x)\\cdot(-3).$$ We want to find the maximum of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (10+\\sqrt3x)\\cdot(-3)$ where $0<x<10$. This function achieves its maximum when $10+\\sqrt3x=\\sqrt{3}(10)$, so it achieves a maximum value of $10\\sqrt3$. $\\boxed{}. \\qquadAPS5505}$",
