    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since the triangle is equilateral and \\(AB = 11\\), the base for the triangle is \\(11\\). Since the triangle can only be placed inside the rectangle, the largest possible height of the triangle is the height of \\(AD\\), which is \\(10\\).\n\nBased on the triangle's isosceles nature, the maximum possible area that the triangle can occupy, within the confines of \\(ABCD\\), is when the triangle's height is equal to the width of \\(ABCD\\). We will derive this area using \\( p \\) representing the length of the width of the rectangle \\(ABCD\\), and \\( h \\) representing the height of the triangle. Since the height \\(h\\) is equal to the width \\(p\\), we can set up the following equation for the sales of based area:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} h p = 10 \\cdot 11\n\\]\nFor the triangle, the maximum length of the base would be applicable when the height \\(h = p\\), so we can relate \\(p\\) to \\(h\\). We know that the sides of the triangle, after rotations around the centroid, must make the triangle an equilateral triangle.\n\nLet's assign \\(11 - p = h / \\sqrt{3}\\) :\n\nNow, we conclude by iterating:\n\n1. Had try to use non-equal-wise triangle-area formula instead. Applying the formula for the largest non-equilateral including equilateral triangle inside the rectangle to solve:\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times (10^2 + 11^2 - 10^2) \\]\nHowever:\n\\[\n\\text{This equals 0, so it simply stops causing infinite iterations}\n\\]\n\nBoth of these problems fall on one key point: ensuring that the area of the triangle equals exactly 100. But, the triangle's isosceles, thus triangle sides that are ~10-over-3 times the ground of the rectangle, allows us to determine that the max possible area within a right triangle would be 62.50.\n\nSo we conclude that:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{62.5}\n\\]",
