    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can be drawn inside a rectangle with sides of lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the placement of the triangle within the rectangle and the possible orientations that maximize its area.\n\n1. **Identify the largest possible equilateral triangle:**\n   An equilateral triangle inscribed in a rectangle will touch the ends of both sides of the rectangle. The side length of such a triangle will be the same as the distance from one side of the rectangle to the opposite side of the triangle, i.e., 10 or 11 units.\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the equilateral triangle:**\n   The area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{s^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4}\n   \\]\n   The closest side length to 14 (half of 28, which is \\(2 \\times 14 = 28\\)) that allows the triangle to fit entirely within the rectangle is 10 feet (as 11 feet is too large). Therefore, the side length of the triangle is 10 feet.\n\n   Substituting \\(s = 10\\) into the formula gives:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{10^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{100 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = 25 \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle that can be drawn inside the rectangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
